139:The per-face slot tree carries wrapped FCK, slot identifiers and nonsecret KDF parameters; it cannot embed a sibling wrapper. Bind/unbind/rotation prove the standing FCK and affect that tree and that face's rows alone. The bundle contains only intended wrapped recovery routes, not a bare FCK or guardian-held secret shares. A passkey/PRF slot stands only after its derivation is measured on target devices (`browser-custody.ts:41-43`). Last browser tab/session close drops bare face keys and returns to F (`stable-founding-road.mem:199,202`); an installed app or worker does not extend authority.
141:''Node S register.'' The present private place signing seed is a named candidate S, with the two unresolved treatments below (`vault-carriers.ts:240-242`; `node-vessel-identity.ts:429-442`). The existing SK tree has *zero* serving payload rows (`vault-carriers.ts:12-14,249-251`), so SK is not a justification for a common unlock. Future ember serving rows enter S only with their own row names, principal, opener and restart rule. No browser, phone, installed web app, tablet, CLI, MCP, watcher or probe owns S (`stable-founding-road.mem:207`). A container inherits the node's actual S set and cannot create one by packaging.
172:# ''iOS phone / Home Screen app:'' suspend or kill the asker during an occupied write. The write waits or completes from an eligible same-face session; it never uses a sibling or infers death from silence. Relaunch starts at F and asks for that face again. ''LEAD:'' device lifecycle must be exercised on the target browser (round 4's YIN handback in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face">>).
173:# ''Android phone / WebAPK:'' kill worker/process and restart; F reads, no D opens before that face unlocks, and A's former worker state cannot open B. Install mode adds no key authority. ''LEAD:'' exercise on target device (round 4's YANG handback in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face">>).
178:# ''Herm / crossroads:'' zero face rows and zero current serving rows; public card/relay service works before any face. No private seed leaks to output. Reboot drops raised ephemeral caps. The place signer's boot behavior follows the OPEN JOINT ruling, not an assumed F classification (`vault-carriers.ts:12-14,240-256`; `stable-founding-road.mem:207`).
224:: ''Owner: the place, not a face.'' The archive is minted at the place card (`ensurePlaceCard`, `init`) from the vessel's own seed before any face stands; a faceless or joinee place holds one. It carries the place Individual's membership and prekey secrets and its contact card. It is a NODE-ONLY secret and enters S as the separately named row `place-archive`. Each face's own archive already rests per PersonaGroup as `veil-archive-g<digest>` (D, ''face-hot'').
231:!!! The place seed's reach (ruled 2026-10-19)
233:* ''The veil leaves the seed.'' `deriveDyadVeil(vesselSeed, groupTag)` (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/dyad.ts`) and `groupSecretOpenerFromSeed` (`packages/lararium-node/src/open-node-vessel.ts`) make the vessel seed an ancestor of every face's device veil and group secret. Each becomes D: the veil mints at random at the join and rests under that face's FCK; the opener reads the face's own key. The isolation red gains the row: a thief holding the place seed derives no veil and opens no group secret of any face.
235:* ''The census sorts by capability, never by host'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19). Each present use of a vessel seed, on node and browser alike, sorts by the capability it serves: a floor duty goes to the place signer, which a vessel holds only while it stands a floor; an act on a face's behalf (a dial, a join, an enrolment, a sibling channel, a daemon proof) goes to that face's atom; nothing else keeps a device-wide key. The browser's device seed (`open-browser-vessel.ts`: the Keyhive Individual, the oracle proof, daemon auth, sibling enrolment, the group-secret opener) and the node's (`open-node-vessel.ts`) take the same sort.
236:* ''The seed's other reach narrows to an allowlist.'' The online place signer serves the floor duties the roadmap's place-signer ruling enumerates; every other present use (the daemon's auth, the device-key signer, the internal asks) either names its domain on that allowlist or takes a key of its own.
